@page "/docs/utilities/scrollbar"
@using Blaizio.Docs.Examples.Scrollbar
<PageTitle>Scrollbar - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Scrollbar</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Utilities</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">CSS only</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p>A slim scrollbar in place of the OS one: a transparent track and a rounded thumb that reads as a hairline at rest and firms up on hover. Its colors come from the theme tokens, so it follows light and dark mode and every preset. Components already mark their scroll areas with <code>scrollbar-thin</code>, but the class does nothing until your project opts in, so an app that never asked keeps its native scrollbars everywhere.</p>
    </header>

    <UsageBlock Example="typeof(ScrollbarUsageExample)"
                Setup="@null"
                Description="Add the class to the overflow container. `scrollbar-hover`, `scrollbar-activity`, `scrollbar-none` and `scrollbar-auto` ship in the stylesheet Blaizio already imports into your Tailwind entry point, while `scrollbar-thin` also needs the project-level opt-in below." />

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Turning it on</h2>
        <p>Pass <code>--scrollbar</code> to <code>blaizio new</code>, <code>blaizio add</code> or <code>blaizio apply</code> and the CLI appends this block to your Tailwind input, or you can paste it yourself. It redefines the empty stub that ships in <code>blaizio.css</code>, so every <code>scrollbar-thin</code> class in the components takes effect at once. Because the block lives in your own file, you restyle the scrollbar by editing it and opt back out by deleting it.</p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@OptInCss" Numbered="false" Language="css" />
        <p>This site opts in the same way, with the block in the docs' own <code>app.css</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <ExamplesHeading />

    <Demo Title="Variants"
          Description="Choose how present the scrollbar is: always, only under the pointer, or only while the box is scrolling. The last variant hides it entirely, which suits a list that has its own scroll controls."
          Example="typeof(ScrollbarVariantsExample)" />

    <Demo Title="Overriding a component's bar"
          Description="Components with a built-in scrollbar, such as select and command lists, menus, the virtualizer and tables, accept an override through `Class`, or `ContainerClass` on a table. All `scrollbar-*` utilities form one class group, so the one you pass replaces the default. To style a scrollbar entirely your own way, pass `scrollbar-auto` alongside your own class."
          Example="typeof(ScrollbarOverrideExample)" />

    <section class="space-y-6">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Class reference</h2>
        <ClassTable Rows="@Classes" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Notes</h2>
        <p><code>scrollbar-activity</code> needs one thing from the host: an <code>is-scrolling</code> class on the container while it is being scrolled. A few lines of your own scroll handler can add it, or a delegated listener for a data attribute can cover the whole app, as this site does.</p>
        <p>Firefox styles the bar through <code>scrollbar-width</code> and <code>scrollbar-color</code>, while Chromium and WebKit use the <code>::-webkit-scrollbar</code> pseudo-elements, which is where the hover effect lives. Both look right, and the hover effect is a progressive enhancement.</p>
        <p><code>scrollbar-auto</code> deliberately declares nothing for <code>::-webkit-scrollbar</code>. A browser paints its own bar only where no author rule has claimed those pseudo-elements, so handing it back means leaving them alone rather than resetting them. A reset renders an invisible bar in a reserved gutter.</p>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    private const string OptInCss =
        """
        @utility scrollbar-thin {
          scrollbar-width: thin;
          scrollbar-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground)) transparent;

          &::-webkit-scrollbar {
            width: 8px;
            height: 8px;
          }
          &::-webkit-scrollbar-track {
            background: transparent;
          }
          &::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
            border-radius: 9999px;
            background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground));
            border: 2px solid transparent;
            background-clip: padding-box;
          }
          &::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb:hover {
            background-color: var(--color-muted-foreground);
          }
        }
        """;

    private static readonly (string, string[], string)[] Classes =
    [
        ("scrollbar-thin",
        [
            "scrollbar-width: thin;",
            "scrollbar-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground)) transparent;",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 8px; height: 8px; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar-track { background: transparent; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { border-radius: 9999px; background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground)); border: 2px solid transparent; background-clip: padding-box; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb:hover { background-color: var(--color-muted-foreground); }",
        ],
        "A slim themed bar, always visible while the content overflows. Project-level opt-in: inert until the block above is in your input."),

        ("scrollbar-hover",
        [
            "scrollbar-width: thin;",
            "scrollbar-color: transparent transparent;",
            "&:hover { scrollbar-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground)) transparent; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 8px; height: 8px; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { border-radius: 9999px; background-color: transparent; border: 2px solid transparent; background-clip: padding-box; }",
            "&:hover::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-border) 70%, var(--color-muted-foreground)); }",
        ],
        "The same bar, revealed only while the pointer is over the container. The gutter stays reserved, so nothing shifts."),

        ("scrollbar-activity",
        [
            "color-scheme: light dark;",
            "scrollbar-width: thin;",
            "scrollbar-color: transparent transparent;",
            "&.is-scrolling { scrollbar-color: var(--color-muted-foreground) transparent; }",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background-color: transparent; transition: background-color 0.4s ease; }",
            "&.is-scrolling::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background-color: var(--color-muted-foreground); }",
        ],
        "Revealed only while the container is being scrolled, fading out shortly after. Needs an is-scrolling class from the host."),

        ("scrollbar-none",
        [
            "scrollbar-width: none;",
            "&::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }",
        ],
        "No bar at all. The content still scrolls, by wheel, touch and keyboard."),

        ("scrollbar-auto",
        [
            "scrollbar-width: auto;",
            "scrollbar-color: auto;",
        ],
        "The browser's own bar. The opt-out, and the clean slate for a bar of your own. It claims no ::-webkit-scrollbar rules, which is what lets the browser paint its default."),
    ];
}
